#+title: Tensor Kernel
#+auto_tangle: t
#+PROPERTY: header-args:zig :tangle ../../src/tensor-code/kernel.zig :mkdirp yes

Tensor-code supplies compact handles for tensor structures that appear in CFT
coefficients.  The expensive work is generation and selection of invariant
tensors, so those procedures should stream candidates rather than building a
large expression tree inside the kernel.

#+begin_src zig
const symmetry = @import("symmetry.zig");
#+end_src

#+interface:

The basic ids are deliberately small.  They name entries in stores owned by a
preset, a tensor generator, or a client.

#+begin_src zig
/// TensorExprId names a tensor expression stored by tensor-code.
pub const TensorExprId = u32;
/// TensorTermId names one term inside a tensor expression store.
pub const TensorTermId = u32;
/// InvariantFamilyId names a family of invariant tensor candidates.
pub const InvariantFamilyId = u16;
/// AlgebraSchemaId names a Lie or superalgebra schema.
pub const AlgebraSchemaId = u16;
/// GradingId names a grading attached to a tensor algebra schema.
pub const GradingId = u16;
#+end_src

Invariant generation should be pull-based.  A caller can feed the source to an
independent-basis selector without materializing every candidate first.

#+begin_src zig
/// TensorExprSource streams tensor expression ids from a generator.
pub const TensorExprSource = struct {
    next_fn: *const fn (*anyopaque) ?TensorExprId,
    state: *anyopaque,

    /// next returns the next generated tensor expression id.
    pub fn next(self: *TensorExprSource) ?TensorExprId {
        return self.next_fn(self.state);
    }
};
#+end_src

An invariant family says which algebra and external representations define the
candidate space.  The actual candidates are produced by a generator.

#+begin_src zig
/// InvariantFamily declares the tensor-invariant problem to generate.
pub const InvariantFamily = struct {
    name: []const u8,
    algebra: AlgebraSchemaId,
    external_representations: []const symmetry.RepresentationId,
};
#+end_src

The algebra schema stores only compact references to core tensors such as the
metric and structure constants.  This is enough for CFT coefficient code to
refer to \(SO(10)\), \(SO(32)\), \(E_8\), or superalgebra data without owning
their full simplifier.

#+begin_src zig
/// AlgebraSchema records the compact tensor data of a symmetry algebra.
pub const AlgebraSchema = struct {
    name: []const u8,
    symmetry: symmetry.SymmetryId,
    grading: ?GradingId = null,
    metric: ?TensorExprId = null,
    structure_constants: ?TensorExprId = null,
};
#+end_src
